    "result_text": "The only test class is `AcceptanceTest`, and the full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the GOLD membership tier rule with minimal structural impact by adding it as a small, cohesive unit at the response edge rather than growing the large `Owner` class or the `OwnerMapper` expression.\n\n**Changes (3 files):**\n\n1. **New `HouseholdTier` helper** (`rest/function/owner/HouseholdTier.java`) \u2014 a tiny stateless class with one static method `isGold(owner, repo)` that returns true when 3+ owners share the owner's `householdId`. Zero surrounding complexity.\n\n2. **`RespondWithOwner`** (the GET responder) \u2014 after mapping, override the tier to `\"GOLD\"` when `HouseholdTier.isGold(...)`. This is the path the test exercises (`getOwner(id)`).\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwnerCreated`** (the POST responder) \u2014 same override, so the create's own response is consistent with \"after this create.\"\n\n**Why this shape:** the existing BRONZE/SILVER logic lives untouched in the `OwnerMapper` expression; GOLD is only ever an override applied when the household reaches 3, so the fallback rules apply exactly as before. I deliberately avoided adding a transient field + getter/setter to `Owner` (a large, high-WMC class) and avoided editing the mapper's complex expression \u2014 either would have paid the whole class's surrounding complexity per the cost model. Each changed responder has WMC_other of ~0 (single-method classes), keeping the total cost low.\n\nThe new `coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` test passes and the full suite is green.",
